            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Texas and Louisiana argue that \u00a7\u202f1226(c) of Title\u202f8 requires the Department of Homeland Security to arrest certain non\u2011citizens when they are released from state prison, and that the Department\u2019s failure to do so imposes costs on the States."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Court",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "df847a51db9cb0bf1f60755966ec656df612a9de7bb7d5cffa3952d957201e37",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-df847a51db9c-0000-0005"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 7,
              "start": 7
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The District Court found that the States would incur costs because of the Department\u2019s guidelines and therefore had standing."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Court",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "df847a51db9cb0bf1f60755966ec656df612a9de7bb7d5cffa3952d957201e37",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-df847a51db9c-0000-0006"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 8,
              "start": 8
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The Supreme Court\u2019s central question was whether the States have Article\u202fIII standing to maintain the suit."
      }
    ],
